# Laravel Domain Rules

## Delivery state machine
- Every delivery state transition MUST be explicitly validated.
- Never assign arbitrary status strings from controllers or clients.
- Persist transition history as delivery events/audit records.
- A transition MUST validate actor, current state, preconditions, and side effects.

## Acceptance and concurrency
- A delivery can be accepted by only one winning driver.
- Multiple requests attempting acceptance MUST be safely serialized or rejected by transactional controls.
- Do not rely on a frontend button disable to prevent double assignment.

## Offers and counter-offers
- An offer is a first-class concept with status and timestamps.
- Counter-offers are first-class records and retain their history.
- Counter-offers become available only after the configured acceptance window expires without assignment.
- A driver may submit another counter-offer after rejection while negotiation remains open.
- Once one offer/counter-offer is accepted, competing proposals MUST be closed according to documented rules.

## Multi-delivery driver behavior
- A driver may have multiple active deliveries.
- Never assume one driver equals one active delivery.
- Do not implement automatic route optimization in MVP unless explicitly requested.

## Cancellation, failure, return
- Cancellation permissions are state-dependent.
- If cancellation occurs after pickup and return is required, the delivery must follow the return flow and business confirmation.
- Delivery failure requires a standardized reason and appropriate evidence according to the business rules.

## Proof of delivery
- Delivery completion requires the configured proof-of-delivery data.
- Do not mark a delivery delivered solely from a client-provided status string.

## Financial invariants
- Final price, commission, refund, and payout values must be validated and persisted server-side.
- Historical financial snapshots must remain immutable except for explicit audited adjustments.
